{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MJqaz2TrgYDYHAntfK6UXK","type":"tool_result","content":"% Related work and context -- drafted by loop0015 item 08.\n\\section{Related work and context}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 08 drafts this section: the human audit of the August 2026 results (arXiv 2608.14673), the working group named in the collection's history, Impagliazzo--Paturi, Impagliazzo--Paturi--Zane, the comparator mechanism, nanoda; ``we found no prior statement'', never ``first''. Ends with what this does not claim''.}\n----REFS\n% Paper 3: references. Only works the paper cites; no width-parameter\n% references (owner's scope, 2026-11-05; ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD entry S4). DOIs\n% checked against Crossref on 2026-11-05 where given; entries without a DOI\n% name the file or URL they were checked against. Item 08 completes the\n% entries it fetches (comparator, nanoda, the human-audit paper's version).\n\n% The manuscript's own BibTeX block, copied verbatim from\n% ~/dev/untrusted/openai-math/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/README.md\n% (lines 10-16). The manuscript's \\date reads \"17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026\".\n@misc{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026,\n  author = {{OpenAI}},\n  title = {{The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False}},\n  howpublished = {OpenAI Math Release preprint\n                  \\href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf}{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}},\n  year = {2026}\n}\n\n% ETH. DOI checked 2026-11-05 (Crossref: \"On the Complexity of k-SAT\", JCSS, 2001-03).\n@article{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001,\n  author  = {Impagliazzo, Russell and Paturi, Ramamohan},\n  title   = {On the complexity of \\emph{k}-{SAT}},\n  journal = {Journal of Computer and System Sciences},\n  volume  = {62}, number = {2}, pages = {367--375}, year = {2001},\n  doi     = {10.1006/jcss.2000.1727}\n}\n\n% The sparsification lemma; SETH's standard setting. DOI checked 2026-11-05\n% (Crossref: \"Which Problems Have Strongly Exponential Complexity?\", JCSS, 2001-12).\n@article{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001,\n  author  = {Impagliazzo, Russell and Paturi, Ramamohan and Zane, Francis},\n  title   = {Which problems have strongly exponential complexity?},\n  journal = {Journal of Computer and System Sciences},\n  volume  = {63}, number = {4}, pages = {512--530}, year = {2001},\n  doi     = {10.1006/jcss.2001.1774}\n}\n\n% The human audit of the ten results of 1 August 2026 (not of family 378).\n% Metadata from the arXiv API on 2026-11-05 (v3, 2026-09-09, 13 pages).\n@misc{SienickiSienicki2026,\n  author        = {Sienicki, Miko{\\l}aj and Sienicki, Krzysztof},\n  title         = {A Human Audit of {OpenAI}'s {AI}-Generated Mathematical Proofs},\n  year          = {2026},\n  eprint        = {2608.14673},\n  archiveprefix = {arXiv},\n  primaryclass  = {cs.AI},\n  note          = {v3, 9 September 2026},\n  url           = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.14673}\n}\n\n% The comparator mechanism the collection uses (its README names the checks:\n% statement match by exported declarations, permitted axioms, kernel replay,\n% the landrun sandbox, enable_nanoda). Item 08 fetches and records the commit\n% and the quoted sentences.\n@misc{LeanComparator,\n  author       = {{Lean FRO}},\n  title        = {comparator: checking a solution against a challenge statement},\n  howpublished = {\\url{https://github.com/leanprover/comparator}},\n  year         = {2026},\n  note         = {Repository README; version and date recorded by item 08}\n}\n\n% nanoda: the independent Lean 4 kernel in Rust that the comparator can replay\n% an exported environment through (enable_nanoda / external_kernels). Item 08\n% records the repository, author and commit it fetched.\n@misc{Nanoda,\n  author       = {{nanoda contributors}},\n  title        = {nanoda: a type checker for {Lean}~4 written in {Rust}},\n  howpublished = {\\url{https://github.com/ammkrn/nanoda_lib}},\n  year         = {2026},\n  note         = {Repository README; author, version and date recorded by item 08}\n}\n\n% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n% Item 05 (Section 6): the sources of the hypotheses, as the manuscript cites\n% them in its §1.1.1-1.1.2. Each entry is copied from the manuscript's own\n% reference list (reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt, the lines named in the\n% note; checked by python3 -I paper3/scripts/consequences.py). No DOI is given:\n% item 05 fetched nothing (TASK.md's download list), so none was checked;\n% item 08 or the owner may add them.\n% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n% Gap-ETH, first source. Extract lines 3711-3712 ([Din16]).\n@techreport{Dinur2016,\n  author      = {Dinur, Irit},\n  title       = {Mildly exponential reduction from {Gap} {3SAT} to polynomial-gap {Label-Cover}},\n  institution = {Electronic Colloquium on Computational Complexity},\n  number      = {TR16-128},\n  year        = {2016},\n  note        = {Entry copied from the manuscript's reference list, extract lines 3711--3712; no DOI checked}\n}\n\n% Gap-ETH, second source. Extract lines 3759-3761 ([MR17]).\n@inproceedings{ManurangsiRaghavendra2017,\n  author    = {Manurangsi, Pasin and Raghavendra, Prasad},\n  title     = {A birthday repetition theorem and complexity of approximating dense {CSPs}},\n  booktitle = {44th International Colloquium on Automata, Languages, and Programming (ICALP 2017)},\n  series    = {LIPIcs},\n  volume    = {80},\n  pages     = {78:1--78:15},\n  year      = {2017},\n  note      = {Entry copied from the manuscript's reference list, extract lines 3759--3761; no DOI checked}\n}\n\n% The randomised Gap-ETH (the manuscript's \"[CCK+20, Conjecture 2.5]\"). Extract lines 3689-3691.\n@article{Chalermsook2020,\n  author  = {Chalermsook, Parinya and Cygan, Marek and Kortsarz, Guy and Laekhanukit, Bundit and Manurangsi, Pasin and Nanongkai, Danupon and Trevisan, Luca},\n  title   = {From {Gap-Exponential} {Time} {Hypothesis} to fixed parameter tractable inapproximability: {Clique}, {Dominating} {Set}, and more},\n  journal = {SIAM Journal on Computing},\n  volume  = {49}, number = {4}, pages = {772--810}, year = {2020},\n  note    = {Entry copied from the manuscript's reference list, extract lines 3689--3691; no DOI checked}\n}\n\n% NSETH. Extract lines 3686-3688 ([CGI+16]).\n@inproceedings{Carmosino2016,\n  author    = {Carmosino, Marco L. and Gao, Jiawei and Impagliazzo, Russell and Mihajlin, Ivan and Paturi, Ramamohan and Schneider, Stefan},\n  title     = {Nondeterministic extensions of the {Strong} {Exponential} {Time} {Hypothesis} and consequences for non-reducibility},\n  booktitle = {7th Innovations in Theoretical Computer Science Conference (ITCS 2016)},\n  pages     = {261--270},\n  year      = {2016},\n  note      = {Entry copied from the manuscript's reference list, extract lines 3686--3688; no DOI checked}\n}\n\n% #ETH and the bounded-error randomised ETH (the manuscript's \"[DHM+14, Section 1]\"). Extract lines 3708-3710.\n@article{Dell2014,\n  author  = {Dell, Holger and Husfeldt, Thore and Marx, D{\\'a}niel and Taslaman, Nina and Wahl{\\'e}n, Martin},\n  title   = {Exponential time complexity of the permanent and the {Tutte} polynomial},\n  journal = {ACM Transactions on Algorithms},\n  volume  = {10}, number = {4}, pages = {21:1--21:32}, year = {2014},\n  note    = {Entry copied from the manuscript's reference list, extract lines 3708--3710; no DOI checked}\n}\n\n% The bounded-error randomised SETH (the manuscript's \"[CDL+16, Definition 2.1]\") and the\n% Set Splitting reduction (its Observation 3.6). Extract lines 3705-3707.\n@article{Cygan2016,\n  author  = {Cygan, Marek and Dell, Holger and Lokshtanov, Daniel and Marx, D{\\'a}niel and Nederlof, Jesper and Okamoto, Yoshio and Paturi, Ramamohan and Saurabh, Saket and Wahlstr{\\\"o}m, Magnus},\n  title   = {On problems as hard as {CNF-SAT}},\n  journal = {ACM Transactions on Algorithms},\n  volume  = {12}, number = {3}, pages = {41:1--41:24}, year = {2016},\n  note    = {Entry copied from the manuscript's reference list, extract lines 3705--3707; no DOI checked}\n}\n\n% MA-SETH refuted in 2016; context only (the manuscript's \"[Wil16, Theorem 3.3]\"). Extract lines 3810-3811.\n@inproceedings{Williams2016,\n  author    = {Williams, Richard Ryan},\n  title     = {Strong {ETH} breaks with {Merlin} and {Arthur}: short non-interactive proofs of batch evaluation},\n  booktitle = {31st Conference on Computational Complexity (CCC 2016)},\n  series    = {LIPIcs},\n  volume    = {50},\n  pages     = {2:1--2:17},\n  year      = {2016},\n  note      = {Entry copied from the manuscript's reference list, extract lines 3810--3811; no DOI checked}\n}\n\n% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n% Item 06 (Section 7): the tools and the instance distribution of the\n% practitioner's measurement. Written from the tools' own metadata (pip show,\n% the package names in paper3/latex/tables/practitioner_meta.json) and from\n% memory for the venues; NO DOI was checked (item 06 may download nothing but\n% pip packages). Item 08 or the owner may check each against Crossref\n% (ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD entry 06c).\n% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n% CaDiCaL, the CDCL solver used (version 1.9.5 through python-sat's \"cadical195\").\n@inproceedings{Biere2024CaDiCaL,\n  author    = {Armin Biere and Tobias Faller and Katalin Fazekas and Mathias Fleury and Nils Froleyks and Florian Pollitt},\n  title     = {{CaDiCaL} 2.0},\n  booktitle = {Computer Aided Verification (CAV 2024)},\n  series    = {Lecture Notes in Computer Science},\n  volume    = {14681},\n  pages     = {133--152},\n  publisher = {Springer},\n  year      = {2024},\n  note      = {Venue from memory; no DOI checked. The version run is 1.9.5, the one python-sat 1.9.dev15 bundles as \\texttt{cadical195}}\n}\n\n% python-sat, the binding through which CaDiCaL is called.\n@inproceedings{Ignatiev2018PySAT,\n  author    = {Alexey Ignatiev and Antonio Morgado and Joao Marques-Silva},\n  title     = {{PySAT}: A {Python} Toolkit for Prototyping with {SAT} Oracles},\n  booktitle = {Theory and Applications of Satisfiability Testing (SAT 2018)},\n  series    = {Lecture Notes in Computer Science},\n  volume    = {10929},\n  pages     = {428--437},\n  publisher = {Springer},\n  year      = {2018},\n  note      = {Venue from memory; no DOI checked. Package python-sat 1.9.dev15, \\url{https://github.com/pysathq/pysat}}\n}\n\n% Ganak, the exact model counter (pyganak 2.8.0, which runs Arjun preprocessing then Ganak).\n@inproceedings{Sharma2019Ganak,\n  author    = {Shubham Sharma and Subhajit Roy and Mate Soos and Kuldeep S. Meel},\n  title     = {{GANAK}: A Scalable Probabilistic Exact Model Counter},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the 28th International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI 2019)},\n  pages     = {1169--1176},\n  year      = {2019},\n  note      = {Venue from memory; no DOI checked. Package pyganak 2.8.0 (Mate Soos), \\url{https://github.com/meelgroup/ganak}}\n}\n\n% ApproxMC, the approximate model counter (pyapproxmc 4.4.0).\n@inproceedings{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\n  author    = {Supratik Chakraborty and Kuldeep S. Meel and Moshe Y. Vardi},\n  title     = {A Scalable Approximate Model Counter},\n  booktitle = {Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2013)},\n  series    = {Lecture Notes in Computer Science},\n  volume    = {8124},\n  pages     = {200--216},\n  publisher = {Springer},\n  year      = {2013},\n  note      = {Venue from memory; no DOI checked. Package pyapproxmc 4.4.0 (Mate Soos), \\url{https://github.com/meelgroup/approxmc}}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{Soos2019BIRD,\n  author    = {Mate Soos and Kuldeep S. Meel},\n  title     = {{BIRD}: Engineering an Efficient {CNF-XOR} {SAT} Solver and Its Applications to Approximate Model Counting},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the 33rd AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2019)},\n  pages     = {1592--1599},\n  year      = {2019},\n  note      = {Venue from memory; no DOI checked. The ApproxMC version run descends from this one}\n}\n\n% The clause ratio 4.26: the empirical threshold of random 3-SAT.\n@article{Selman1996Hard,\n  author  = {Bart Selman and David G. Mitchell and Hector J. Levesque},\n  title   = {Generating Hard Satisfiability Problems},\n  journal = {Artificial Intelligence},\n  volume  = {81},\n  number  = {1--2},\n  pages   = {17--29},\n  year    = {1996},\n  note    = {Venue from memory; no DOI checked}\n}\n\n@article{Mertens2006Threshold,\n  author  = {Stephan Mertens and Marc M{\\'e}zard and Riccardo Zecchina},\n  title   = {Threshold Values of Random {K-SAT} from the Cavity Method},\n  journal = {Random Structures \\& Algorithms},\n  volume  = {28},\n  number  = {3},\n  pages   = {340--373},\n  year    = {2006},\n  note    = {Venue from memory; no DOI checked. Puts the 3-SAT threshold at $4.2667$}\n}\n----AMB\n# ETH_AMBIGUOUS — questions the loop decided for itself, for the owner\n\n**Protocol** (owner, 2026-11-05). Paper 3 is written by Ralph loop0015\nwith no stopping midway. Whenever an item finds something ambiguous — a\nwording, a claim, a citation form, a choice between two sound ways of\ndoing a thing — it **decides**, writes **one entry here per question**,\nand continues. Each entry has three parts: *what was ambiguous*, *what\nthe item chose*, *what the owner may want to change*. Entries are dated\nand numbered (`S` for setup, then the item number and a letter: `01a`,\n`01b`, …). Nothing here changes the title, which is fixed:\n\n> Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.\n\nItem 09 lists in the paper's conclusion the entries that affect the text;\nitem 11 lists all of them in `README.md`. The owner resolves an entry by\nediting the paper and striking the entry (or by leaving it, which is also\nan answer).\n\n## Entries\n\n### S1 — the author line (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: who the authors are. Paper 1 is Linhares and Yanasse;\n  the brief for this paper names \"Alexandre Linhares\" and \"the MOSP\n  project\" as a placeholder.\n- **Chosen**: `\\author{Alexandre Linhares \\and the MOSP project}` in\n  `latex/main.tex`, with a comment marking the second author as a\n  placeholder; the acknowledgements will carry the AI-use disclosure in\n  paper 1's form.\n- **Owner may change**: the second author (Yanasse? none? a project\n  name?), affiliations, ORCID.\n\n### S2 — `2^n` in the typeset title (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: the title string carries the plain-text `2^n`; LaTeX\n  cannot typeset `^` in text mode, and a typeset title would ordinarily\n  show a superscript.\n- **Chosen**: `\\title{... all $2^n$ assignments ...}` (a superscript `n`\n  in the PDF) with `\\ldots` for the ellipsis; the PDF metadata\n  (`pdftitle`) carries the plain string with the caret literal (`2\\string^n`, since hyperref drops a bare `^` from PDF strings); the plain\n  string also sits in a comment directly above `\\title`. The gate strips\n  `$` and `\\ldots` before comparing. Nothing else in the title is\n  touched.\n- **Owner may change**: typeset the caret literally (`2\\textasciicircum\n  n`) if the plain form is wanted on the page.\n\n### S3 — where the companion Lean lives (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: the restatement `SubexpCount` and the verbatim machine\n  model are under `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/`, a path\n  named for loop0014's pathwidth question, which this paper does not\n  touch. Cite them there, or move them to a neutral path?\n- **Chosen**: item 07 moves them — `PrimitiveMachine.lean` and the\n  ETH-only declarations of `Frame