{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fgr3bZWZACMnjPaupmbfNf","type":"tool_result","content":"4eaee8754\n----HIST\n18:- *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False.* The paper gives a deterministic algorithm that exactly counts satisfying assignments of fixed-width CNF formulas in subexponential time and polynomial space. The case of 3-SAT refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis. An independent working group of computer scientists led by Russell Impagliazzo, Ramamohan Paturi, and Ryan Williams will examine and explain the proof. At the group’s request, we are making the materials public immediately so others can examine them as well.\n----378\n     1\t# The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False\n     2\t\n     3\tThe following describes the scope of the Lean formalization related to the following accompanying paper(s):\n     4\t\n     5\t- [The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False](../../preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf)\n     6\t\n     7\t## Scope\n     8\t\n     9\tFor fixed $k\\ge2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly counts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$ variables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$, where $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has visited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.\n    10\t\n    11\tFor $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m\\le Dn$ for fixed $D>0$, this improves to $(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.\n    12\t\n    13\t## Comparator links\n    14\t\n    15\t| Result | Comparator statement |\n    16\t| --- | --- |\n    17\t| Exact counting and SAT on canonical inputs in subexponential time and polynomial space | [SubexponentialSAT.lean](../ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean) |\n----CCREADME\n     1\t# [Comparator](https://github.com/leanprover/comparator) challenges\n     2\t\n     3\tInstall `comparator`, `landrun`, and `lean4export`, and make them available on `PATH`.\n     4\tThen, from `lean/`:\n     5\t\n     6\t```sh\n     7\tlake update\n     8\tlake exe cache get\n     9\tlake env comparator ComparatorChallenges/QuasiRiemannHypothesis.json\n    10\t```\n\n  10 lean/ComparatorChallenges/README.md\n  56 history.md\n  66 total\nShell cwd was reset to /home/al/dev/MOSP","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5aa49e59-911f-4515-a526-615b7175ef9d","uuid":"d048efbc-c191-4ed6-8b18-91fe34d9e1ac","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:58:37.607Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"4eaee8754\n----HIST\n18:- *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False.* The paper gives a deterministic algorithm that exactly counts satisfying assignments of fixed-width CNF formulas in subexponential time and polynomial space. The case of 3-SAT refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis. An independent working group of computer scientists led by Russell Impagliazzo, Ramamohan Paturi, and Ryan Williams will examine and explain the proof. At the group’s request, we are making the materials public immediately so others can examine them as well.\n----378\n     1\t# The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False\n     2\t\n     3\tThe following describes the scope of the Lean formalization related to the following accompanying paper(s):\n     4\t\n     5\t- [The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False](../../preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf)\n     6\t\n     7\t## Scope\n     8\t\n     9\tFor fixed $k\\ge2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly counts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$ variables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$, where $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has visited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.\n    10\t\n    11\tFor $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m\\le Dn$ for fixed $D>0$, this improves to $(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.\n    12\t\n    13\t## Comparator links\n    14\t\n    15\t| Result | Comparator statement |\n    16\t| --- | --- |\n    17\t| Exact counting and SAT on canonical inputs in subexponential time and polynomial space | [SubexponentialSAT.lean](../ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean) |\n----CCREADME\n     1\t# [Comparator](https://github.com/leanprover/comparator) challenges\n     2\t\n     3\tInstall `comparator`, `landrun`, and `lean4export`, and make them available on `PATH`.\n     4\tThen, from `lean/`:\n     5\t\n     6\t```sh\n     7\tlake update\n     8\tlake exe cache get\n     9\tlake env comparator ComparatorChallenges/QuasiRiemannHypothesis.json\n    10\t```\n\n  10 lean/ComparatorChallenges/README.md\n  56 history.md\n  66 total","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /home/al/dev/MOSP","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Fgr3bZWZACMnjPaupmbfNf","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
