{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018sjV4r6WH3cpp3DNpLmnRa","type":"tool_result","content":"**Title:** \"A Human Audit of OpenAIs AI-Generated Mathematical Proofs\"\n\n**Authors:** Mikołaj Sienicki, Krzysztof Sienicki\n\n**Submission history:**\n- [v1] Mon, 3 Aug 2026 15:12:46 UTC (7 KB)\n- [v2] Tue, 25 Aug 2026 03:36:45 UTC (11 KB)\n- [v3] Wed, 9 Sep 2026 12:39:56 UTC (16 KB)\n\n**Subjects:** Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); General Literature (cs.GL); Quantum Physics (quant-ph). The primary class appears to be cs.AI.\n\n**Comments:** \"8 pages, 5 references. V2: 11 pages, 8 references. V3. 13 pages, 27 references. Revised and updated version\"\n\n**Abstract:** The abstract is too long to quote in full under the 125-character limit per quote, so here is a summary in my own words. The paper assesses 18 chapter-specific reviews of ten mathematical results OpenAI announced on 1 August 2026. It does not claim to fully reconstruct all ten proofs. It finds no confirmed substantive error in a principal result, though review depth varies. Chapter 8 draws the strongest reservation, with a specialist requesting major revision. An apparent polarity error in Chapter 6 was withdrawn after a lost overbar was recovered from the typeset source. Subsequent research reuses the Chapter 3 mechanism and confirms Connes's rigidity conjecture is false. Chapter 7 gets the strongest direct corroboration from a follow-up result. The authors argue that confidence should combine formal checking, human reconstruction, independent use, and a public correction record.\n\nFor the full verbatim abstract, see the arXiv abstract page (arxiv.org/abs/2608.14673) or the PDF."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5aa49e59-911f-4515-a526-615b7175ef9d","uuid":"c410987e-6852-4b63-9ed8-dbc6dd5865ed","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:58:49.259Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":41874,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Title:** \"A Human Audit of OpenAIs AI-Generated Mathematical Proofs\"\n\n**Authors:** Mikołaj Sienicki, Krzysztof Sienicki\n\n**Submission history:**\n- [v1] Mon, 3 Aug 2026 15:12:46 UTC (7 KB)\n- [v2] Tue, 25 Aug 2026 03:36:45 UTC (11 KB)\n- [v3] Wed, 9 Sep 2026 12:39:56 UTC (16 KB)\n\n**Subjects:** Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); General Literature (cs.GL); Quantum Physics (quant-ph). The primary class appears to be cs.AI.\n\n**Comments:** \"8 pages, 5 references. V2: 11 pages, 8 references. V3. 13 pages, 27 references. Revised and updated version\"\n\n**Abstract:** The abstract is too long to quote in full under the 125-character limit per quote, so here is a summary in my own words. The paper assesses 18 chapter-specific reviews of ten mathematical results OpenAI announced on 1 August 2026. It does not claim to fully reconstruct all ten proofs. It finds no confirmed substantive error in a principal result, though review depth varies. Chapter 8 draws the strongest reservation, with a specialist requesting major revision. An apparent polarity error in Chapter 6 was withdrawn after a lost overbar was recovered from the typeset source. Subsequent research reuses the Chapter 3 mechanism and confirms Connes's rigidity conjecture is false. Chapter 7 gets the strongest direct corroboration from a follow-up result. The authors argue that confidence should combine formal checking, human reconstruction, independent use, and a public correction record.\n\nFor the full verbatim abstract, see the arXiv abstract page (arxiv.org/abs/2608.14673) or the PDF.","durationMs":6554,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.14673"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018sjV4r6WH3cpp3DNpLmnRa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
