{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019LagH7svFDNWeAGys4PFr7","type":"tool_result","content":"% The statement in our Lean -- drafted by loop0015 item 07 (2026-11-05).\n% Every number here is printed by paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py (the copy, the\n% statement match, the sorry count; log paper3/lean/rediff.log) or by\n% `cd lean && lake env lean ../paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean` (the axiom audit;\n% log paper3/lean/axiom_check.log). Line numbers in our files are at the\n% repository commit the log names; line numbers in the clone are at 4eaee875.\n\\section{The statement in our Lean}\n\\label{sec:lean}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement} and \\ref{sec:proof} read OpenAI's statement\nin OpenAI's files. This section describes the one thing we wrote in Lean\nourselves: a copy of the statement inside our own library, checked by the\nsame kernel, under our own name. The point is modest. Every sentence this\npaper says about ``the verified statement'' is pinned to a named object\nthat the Lean kernel has elaborated, and anyone who wants to use the\nstatement as a hypothesis later can cite that one name. Nothing is proved\nabout the hypothesis here, and nothing is claimed beyond what the copy\nand its audit show.\n\nTwo words first. In Lean a \\emph{Prop} is a sentence that may be true or\nfalse; a \\emph{def} gives a sentence a name without asserting it; a\n\\emph{theorem} asserts a sentence and carries its proof; an \\emph{axiom}\nasserts one without a proof. Our restatement is a def. It names the\nsentence and asserts nothing.\n\nThe files are \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean}\nand \\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean}, written\non 2026-11-04 for an earlier study of ours on a different question. The\ndirectory carries that study's name. The owner chose to leave the files\nwhere they are rather than move them (\\repofile{paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD},\nS3), and this paper cites nothing from that study: only the copied model,\nthe restatement, and six small lemmas about them, all listed in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean}.\n\n\\subsection{The copy: definitions only}\n\\label{sec:lean-copy}\n\nThe comparator file of family~378,\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}, has 220\nlines. Lines 1--190 define the objects the theorem speaks about: the\nformulas, their canonical encoding as a bit string, the multitape machine,\nits step and its starting configuration, the running-time bound\n$\\runtimeScale$ and the wrapper around it, the count word, and the\nlocated configuration that records which tape cells a run has visited.\nLines 191--219 state the theorem \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and end\nit with \\texttt{sorry}, the placeholder that a solution must replace.\nLine 220 closes the last namespace.\n\nOur \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.lean} is lines 1--190 and 220, and nothing\nelse: after a thirty-line header that records the source, the commit, the\nfile's hash and the Apache licence, the file is the copy\n\\leanref{copy-model}. The header says so, and a script checks it.\n\\repofile{paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py} reads both files with\n\\texttt{open()} only, takes everything in ours from the line\n\\texttt{import Mathlib} on, and compares it byte for byte with the clone's\nlines 1--190 followed by line 220. The diff is empty: 191 lines on each\nside, identical. The script also checks the clone file's SHA-256 against\nthe hash the header records, and that the clone is at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}. The log is \\repofile{paper3/lean/rediff.log}.\n\nSo the copy holds the theorem's \\emph{vocabulary} and not the theorem.\nThe \\texttt{sorry} at line 218 is outside the copied range, and our file\nhas no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script\ncounts them: zero and zero in each of our two files). Everything in the\ncopy is a definition. A definition can be badly chosen, but it cannot be\nfalse; what the definitions mean is Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s\nsubject, and the copy adds nothing to it.\n\n\\subsection{The restatement}\n\\label{sec:lean-restatement}\n\n\\texttt{Frame.lean} defines\n\\[\n  \\SubexpCount\\ (k : \\mathbb{N}) : \\mathrm{Prop}\n\\]\nas the body of the comparator theorem for width $k$, with the theorem's\ntwo binders handled thus: $k$ is the argument of the def, and the\nhypothesis $2 \\le k$ is carried by a second def,\n\\texttt{ExactCountAndSat}, which says ``for every $k \\ge 2$,\n----README\n13:## What the paper is\n53:## Layout\n64:## How to build\n77:## How to regenerate every number\n216:## The replay\n241:## Open questions for the owner\n245:## The CLAUDE.md paragraph\n197:- **Section 8, the statement in our Lean** (item 07): `python3 -I\n198-  paper3/scripts/lean_rediff.py` (from the repository root; `--check`\n199-  verifies without writing) re-diffs the copy — our\n200-  `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean` lines 31–221\n201-  against the clone's comparator file lines 1–190 followed by 220, byte for\n202-  byte (identical, 191 lines each side), with the clone's SHA-256\n203-  (`4183b205…`) and commit (`4eaee875`) — and the restatement — the body of\n204-  `SubexpCount` (Frame.lean 85–107) against the comparator theorem's body\n205-  (clone 195–217): identical after whitespace normalisation (1,073\n206-  characters) and line by line after stripping indentation (23 lines) — and\n207-  counts `sorry` and `axiom` declarations in our two files (zero and zero;\n208-  the clone's one `sorry` at line 218 is outside the copied range); log\n209-  `paper3/lean/rediff.log`. The axiom audit is `cd lean && lake env lean","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5aa49e59-911f-4515-a526-615b7175ef9d","uuid":"ed626f59-8b46-4058-81db-38cf0337cd27","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:58:52.680Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"% The statement in our Lean -- drafted by loop0015 item 07 (2026-11-05).