{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NX4q9WuF5L8jj7eXTqkuxt","type":"tool_result","content":"## The OpenAI mathematics collection (loop0014 item 02, 2026-11-04)\n\nNot missing — held locally, read-only, and recorded here because one file\nof it is now copied into this development.\n\n- **OpenAI** (2026). *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False*, family 378\n  of the OpenAI mathematics collection (`github.com/openai/math`,\n  Apache-2.0), manuscript of 17 October 2026 revised 25 October 2026, 64\n  pages; the Lean comparator statement\n  `lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean` (theorem\n  `exact_count_and_sat`, proved in `lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/`).\n  Cloned read-only at `~/dev/untrusted/openai-math` (commit `4eaee875`,\n  2026-10-29); the paper is read as a `pdftotext` extract\n  (`reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/01_audit/eth.txt`, git-ignored) and\n  the Lean files with `cat`; nothing in the clone is built or run, and\n  nothing is imported from it. **Copied verbatim**, under its Apache-2.0\n  header: lines 1–190 and 220 of the comparator file (the definitions —\n  the CNF syntax, canonical encoding, `PrimitiveMachine` model, `Trace`,\n  located configurations, `runtimeScale`, `runtimeBound`; file SHA-256\n  `4183b2057cff350d66848215ff7167196dfc20e1891b598fdddea59077a76685`) into\n  `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean`. The\n  theorem itself is **not** copied: it is restated as the named `Prop`\n  `SubexpCount` in `SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean` and never asserted\n  (`reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §2). The verified exponent is\n  `n (log log n)² / √log n`; the paper's sharper exponents are paper-only\n  (§1.0).\n\n## Pathwidth, cutwidth and edge search on bounded-degree graphs (loop0014 item 07, 2026-11-05)\n\nThe classical ties between pathwidth and cutwidth that `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md`\n----REPLAYVERS\n11:\\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false} in the comparator's JSON\n99:The comparator's JSON for the family has \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}:\n102:(\\repofile{paper3/replay/nanoda.sh}, recorded in the README before it\n103:ran): \\texttt{lean4export} (tag \\texttt{v4.34.0}, commit\n109:\\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} \\citep{Nanoda} (version 0.4.31, commit\n115:(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/nanoda_check.txt}):\n124:\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/export_sha256.txt}) stays in the\n136:  theorem's dependencies as exported, but adds \\texttt{lean4export}\n137:  (that it exports what the environment holds) and \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib}\n142:  \\texttt{lean4export}; we did not run that tool chain, and the\n----NOCLAIM\n367:\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n368-\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n369-\n370-\\begin{itemize}\n371-\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n372-  refuted}, for any problem, any parameter or any constant $c$. Such a\n373-  statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n374-  value, which is unknown.\n375-\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n376-  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n377-  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n378-  problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n379-\\item \\textbf{The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched}, and so is\n380-  the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, and so is every lower bound proved\n381-  from either; the verified statement is for each fixed $k$ and says\n----378quote\n19:in full (\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, lines 9 and 11):\n----READMEtail\n## The replay\n\nDone (item 01, 2026-11-05): `replay/README.md` has the exact scripts,\nthe timeline, every count and hash. In one paragraph: a fresh copy of\nthe clone (commit `4eaee875`) was built inside a `bwrap` sandbox\n(read-only system directories, a private home, the repository\ninvisible, PID/IPC/UTS namespaces, eight cores at `nice -n 19`, network\non for the lakefile's dependency clones and the Mathlib cache) at the\npinned `leanprover/lean4:v4.34.1` with upstream Mathlib\n`d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612`: `lake build\nOAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main` compiled **3,091 modules** (the\naudit's closure exactly) in **33 min** with **0 `error` lines and 0\n`sorry`** lines, and `#print axioms` of\n`SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat` printed\n`[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`. A first run (01:03) failed\nat once on a `lake build -j 8` option this Lake does not have; the\nsecond (01:16) is the one reported. Then a **second kernel**:\n`lean4export` (tag `v4.34.0`, built with `v4.34.1`) exported the theorem\nand its dependencies (2.36 GB, 43.6 M lines) and `nanoda_lib`\n(commit `8de7e0c4`, v0.4.31) checked them: `Checked 95176 declarations\nwith no errors`, three axioms, exit 0, in 110 s. Regenerate the counts\nwith `bash paper3/replay/counts.sh`; the full build log and the export\nstay in `~/dev/untrusted/replay/logs/` (hashes committed under\n`replay/logs/run2/` and `replay/logs/nanoda/`).\n\n## Open questions for the owner\n\nSee `ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD`; item 11 lists the entries here by number.\n\n## The CLAUDE.md paragraph\n\n*(Item 11 drafts it here. Not applied to `CLAUDE.md`; the owner folds it.)*","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5aa49e59-911f-4515-a526-615b7175ef9d","uuid":"e4e1e0a5-48e7-4488-bd70-c87b916d1042","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:59:21.354Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## The OpenAI mathematics collection (loop0014 item 02, 2026-11-04)\n\nNot missing — held locally, read-only, and recorded here because one file\nof it is now copied into this development.\n\n- **OpenAI** (2026). *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False*, family 378\n  of the OpenAI mathematics collection (`github.com/openai/math`,\n  Apache-2.0), manuscript of 17 October 2026 revised 25 October 2026, 64\n  pages; the Lean comparator statement\n  `lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean` (theorem\n  `exact_coun