{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HL3i4gamZhiuJotofKKcNT","type":"tool_result","content":"<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>\n<feed xmlns:opensearch=\"http://a9.com/-/spec/opensearch/1.1/\" xmlns:arxiv=\"http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom\">\n  <id>https://arxiv.org/api/Tjad+Kq2i20MHJQ0natBbRp1msk</id>\n  <title>arXiv Query: search_query=&amp;id_list=2608.14673&amp;start=0&amp;max_results=10</title>\n  <updated>2026-11-05T05:59:12Z</updated>\n  <link href=\"https://arxiv.org/api/query?search_query=&amp;start=0&amp;max_results=10&amp;id_list=2608.14673\" type=\"application/atom+xml\"/>\n  <opensearch:itemsPerPage>10</opensearch:itemsPerPage>\n  <opensearch:totalResults>1</opensearch:totalResults>\n  <opensearch:startIndex>0</opensearch:startIndex>\n  <entry>\n    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2608.14673v3</id>\n    <title>A Human Audit of OpenAIs AI-Generated Mathematical Proofs</title>\n    <updated>2026-09-09T12:39:56Z</updated>\n    <link href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.14673v3\" rel=\"alternate\" type=\"text/html\"/>\n    <link href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.14673v3\" rel=\"related\" type=\"application/pdf\" title=\"pdf\"/>\n    <summary>We assess 18 chapter-specific reviews of the ten mathematical results announced by OpenAI on 1 August 2026, alongside review standards, Lean formalizations, subsequent research, and mathematical references. The article audits this review record without claiming a complete reconstruction of all ten proofs. No confirmed substantive mathematical error in a principal result remains in the examined assessments, although review depth varies and some dependencies remain partly checked. Chapter 8 presents the strongest reservation: a specialist review requests major revision of compressed analytic arguments. In Chapter 6, an apparent polarity error was withdrawn after an overbar lost during PDF extraction was recovered from the typeset source. Subsequent research independently reuses the Chapter 3 proof mechanism and confirms that Connes's rigidity conjecture is false, without independently reproducing Chapter 4's stronger infinite-family result. Among the cited follow-ups, Chapter 7 receives the strongest direct theorem-level corroboration through a stronger hardness theorem. Related equality results in Chapter 8 do not verify the analytic inequality proof. Some follow-ups disclose material AI assistance. We argue that confidence should combine formal checking, human reconstruction, independent mathematical use, and a public record supporting correction of both proofs and reviews.</summary>\n    <category term=\"cs.AI\" scheme=\"http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom\"/>\n    <category term=\"cs.GL\" scheme=\"http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom\"/>\n    <category term=\"quant-ph\" scheme=\"http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom\"/>\n    <published>2026-08-03T15:12:46Z</published>\n    <arxiv:comment>8 pages, 5 references. V2: 11 pages, 8 references. V3. 13 pages, 27 references. Revised and updated version</arxiv:comment>\n    <arxiv:primary_category term=\"cs.AI\"/>\n    <author>\n      <name>Mikołaj Sienicki</name>\n    </author>\n    <author>\n      <name>Krzysztof Sienicki</name>\n    </author>\n  </entry>\n</feed>\n----\n     1\t# Comparator\n     2\t## Deprecation\n     3\tThis project has been merged into core as of Lean release v4.35.0 in the form of `lake comparator`\n     4\tand the accompanying `lake check`. Users are advised to migrate their validations setups to these\n     5\ttools as they are the only ones guaranteed to receive further security and performance improvements.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Introduction\n     8\tComparator is a trustworthy judge for Lean proofs. It relies on having an existing Lean installation as\n     9\twell as:\n    10\t1. [`landrun`](https://github.com/Zouuup/landrun), compiled from the `main` branch's source, present in `PATH`\n    11\t2. [`lean4export`](https://github.com/leanprover/lean4export/), at a version that is compatible with whatever Lean version your project is targeting, present in `PATH`\n    12\t3. (optional) [nanoda](https://github.com/ammkrn/nanoda_lib/), compiled with a recent version of Rust.\n    13\t   This is only necessary if you want to check with the nanoda kernel in addition to the builtin one.\n    14\t   `cargo build --release` will place `nanoda_bin` in the `target/release` directory of the checked-out directory,\n    15\t   this directory must be present in `PATH`\n    16\t\n    17\t> [!NOTE]\n    18\t> Alternatively full paths to these binaries can be specified using the environment variables\n    19\t> `COMPARATOR_LANDRUN`, `COMPARATOR_LEAN4EXPORT`, and `COMPARATOR_NANODA` when invoking Comparator.\n    20\t\n    21\tComparator is configured through a JSON file:\n    22\t```\n    23\t{\n    24\t    \"challenge_module\": \"Challenge\",\n    25\t    \"solution_module\": \"Solution\",\n    26\t    \"theorem_names\": [\"todo1\"],\n    27\t    \"permitted_axioms\": [\"propext\", \"Quot.sound\", \"Classical.choice\"]\n    28\t}\n    29\t```\n    30\tWhere `Challenge.lean` contains at least a theorem named `todo1` that has a `sorry` (or any other proof)\n    31\tand `Solution.lean` is provided by a party trying to convince you that they have proven `todo1` by\n    32\twriting out the same theorem but with a proper proof attached.\n    33\t\n    34\tGiven the following assumptions:\n    35\t1. The transitive closure of imports of `Challenge.lean` as well as `lakefile.toml`/`lakefile.lean`\n    36\t   are controlled by you or trustworthy.\n    37\t2. You have not previously tried to compile the `Solution` file or any other potentially adversarial\n    38\t   files (as that might compromise your `Challenge` file to make it seem like you are looking for a\n    39\t   different proof than you actually are)\n    40\t3. You have the `landrun` and `lean4export` binary in `PATH`\n    41\t4. `landrun` works correctly on your system and `Solution.lean` does not\n    42\t   exploit any bugs in `landrun` that allow a process to escape its sandbox\n    43\t5. The Lean kernel is correct (with `external_kernels` this can be reduced to\n    44\t   \"At least one of the Lean kernel or the `external_kernels` is correct\")\n    45\t6. You are not running this under a privileged user\n    46\t\n    47\tIf the following command succeeds:\n    48\t```\n    49\tsystemd-run --property=RestrictAddressFamilies=~AF_UNIX --user --pty -E PATH=\"$PATH\" --working-directory $(pwd) -- bash -c 'lake env path/to/comparator/binary path/to/config.json'\n    50\t```\n    51\t\n    52\tAll theorems in `Solution` that are listed in `theorem_names` are guaranteed to:\n    53\t1. Prove the same statement as provided in `Challenge`\n    54\t2. Use no more axioms than listed in `permitted_axioms`\n    55\t3. Be accepted by the Lean kernel\n    56\t\n    57\t> [!NOTE]\n    58\t> The Trusted Code Base of Landrun naturally includes the operating system and hardware it is running on, plus its sandboxing mechanism.\n    59\t> The systemd-run part explicitly guard against a vulnerability in landrun, Comparator's current sandboxing solution, that will be fixed in Linux 7.1\n    60\t\n    61\tNote that running `lake exe cache get` to download a Mathlib cache is acceptable before running the\n    62\tcomparator if you trust the cache to not be modified as to, e.g. contain different definitions from\n    63\tthe one you would expect.\n    64\t\n    65\tFurthermore, it is possible to avoid trusting `landrun`'s ability to sandbox the `Solution.lean` file:\n    66\tif you have obtained a fully pre-built `.lake` directory through other means and without compromising your\n    67\tchecking environment, `Solution.lean` will not be rebuilt.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Checking with Additional Kernels\n    70\tComparator can additionally check solutions with external kernels. To do this you must register them\n    71\tin the `external_kernels` list:\n    72\t```\n    73\t{\n    74\t    \"challenge_module\": \"Challenge\",\n    75\t    \"solution_module\": \"Solution\",\n    76\t    \"theorem_names\": [\"todo1\"],\n    77\t    \"permitted_axioms\": [\"propext\", \"Quot.sound\", \"Classical.choice\"],\n    78\t    \"external_kernels\": {\n    79\t        \"mykernel\": [\"kernel_bin\", \"--threads=4\", \"--paranoid\"]\n    80\t    }\n    81\t}\n    82\t```\n    83\tComparator will execute the command described by the `mykernel` array and additionally pass a\n    84\tfile, containing the solution export to the kernel, in this case: `kernel_bin --threads=4 --paranoid export.ndjson`\n    85\t\n    86\tFor backwards compatibility reasons users may instead set `enable_nanoda: true` to obtain a config\n    87\tthat calls `nanoda_bin`. Furthermore, comparator currently attempts to detect `nanoda`-style kernels\n    88\tby checking whether the name contains the string `noda` and instead passing a `nanoda`-style\n    89\t`config.json` to them. This is only intended as a migration path while the kernel ecosystem\n    90\tmoves toward having an option to receive the input file as a `CLI` argument.\n    91\t\n    92\tFor development purposes, comparator supports overriding `nanoda` specifically using the\n    93\t`COMPARATOR_NANODA` environment variable.\n    94\t## Definition Holes\n    95\tSometimes challenges want to leave open definitions for solutions to fill in. This can range from\n    96\tsimple things like filling in a `Prop` valued definition to resolve whether a conjecture is true or\n    97\tfalse, all the way to constructing complex mathematical objects. For these types of solutions,\n    98\tcomparator can guarantee that:\n    99\t1. They use no more axioms than listed in `permitted_axioms`\n   100\t2. They are accepted by the Lean kernel\n   101\t3. The name, type, universe levels and safety levels of all definition holes match\n   102\t\n   103\tCrucially, many definition hole challenges can be gamed without additional oversight.\n   104\tFor example, given a conjecture-style challenge:\n   105\t```lean\n   106\tdef ChallengeSolution : Prop := sorry\n   107\ttheorem challenge : RiemannHypothesis ↔ ChallengeSolution := sorry\n   108\t```\n   109\ta solution could define `ChallengeSolution` as:\n   110\t```lean\n   111\tdef ChallengeSolution : Prop := RiemannHypothesis\n   112\t```\n   113\tand conduct a simple proof of `challenge` by reflexivity. The intention of the challenge though was\n   114\tof course to ask for a `True` or `False` value for `ChallengeSolution`. For this reason, all\n   115\tdefinition hole solutions **must** always be checked with an additional (potentially human)\n   116\tverifier.\n   117\t\n   118\tTo establish a definition hole, the challenge must provide it as a sorried definition:\n   119\t```lean\n   120\tdef large : Nat := sorry\n   121\t\n   122\ttheorem large_lt : 37 < large := sorry\n   123\t```\n   124\tAll of the holes must then be put into the `definition_names` field in `configuration.json`:\n   125\t```\n   126\t{\n   127\t    \"challenge_module\": \"Challenge\",\n   128\t    \"solution_module\": \"Solution\",\n   129\t    \"theorem_names\": [\"large_lt\"],\n   130\t    \"definition_names\": [\"large\"],\n   131\t    \"permitted_axioms\": [\"propext\", \"Quot.sound\", \"Classical.choice\"]\n   132\t}\n   133\t```\n   134\tFor all `definition_names`, comparator ensures that in the solution:\n   135\t- the name, type, universe levels and safety level match\n   136\t- the constant does not (transitively) refer to non-permitted axioms\n   137\t- the constant type checks\n   138\t\n   139\tThus, the following solution would be accepted:\n   140\t```lean\n   141\tdef large : Nat := 38\n   142\t\n   143\ttheorem large_lt : 37 < large := by decide\n   144\t```\n   145\t\n   146\t## Development\n   147\t\n   148\tThe `scripts/fake-landrun.sh` can be used to replace Landrun in development if you are not on a Linux system that supports landrun.\n   149\t\n   150\tThe following commands, starting from the root directory of a fresh git checkout, will build and run `comparator` on one of the test examples:\n   151\t\n   152\t```sh\n   153\tlake build lean4export comparator\n   154\t\n   155\tcd tests/projects/simple_mismatch\n   156\t\n   157\tcat > lakefile.toml <<EOF\n   158\tname = \"comparatortest\"\n   159\tversion = \"0.1.0\"\n   160\t\n   161\t[[lean_lib]]\n   162\tname = \"Solution\"\n   163\t\n   164\t[[lean_lib]]\n   165\tname = \"Challenge\"\n   166\tEOF\n   167\t\n   168\tCOMPARATOR_LANDRUN=$(realpath ../../../scripts/fake-landrun.sh) COMPARATOR_LEAN4EXPORT=$(realpath ../../../.lake/packages/lean4export/.lake/build/bin/lean4export) lake env ../../../.lake/build/bin/comparator config.json\n   169\t```\n   170\t\n   171\tThe following commands, starting from the root directory of a fresh git checkout, will build and run the tests:\n   172\t\n   173\t```sh\n   174\tlake build lean4export comparator\n   175\tCOMPARATOR_LANDRUN=$(realpath scripts/fake-landrun.sh) COMPARATOR_LEAN4EXPORT=$(realpath .lake/packages/lean4export/.lake/build/bin/lean4export) lean --run runtests.lean\n   176\t```\n   177\t\n   178\tReplace the `landrun` and `lean4export` arguments as needed, or place the binaries in `PATH`.\n   179\t\n   180\t## Internals\n   181\tWe generally adopt a policy of not loading olean files as they just get mmaped into our address\n   182\tspace and then dereferenced and are as such a potential point of attack for sophisticated adversaries.\n   183\t\n   184\tThe comparator performs the following steps to ensure these properties:\n   185\t1. Build `Challenge` using `lake` in a `landrun` sandbox that has:\n   186\t   - read access to the entire file system and write access to `/dev`\n   187\t   - write access to the `.lake` directory of the project\n   188\t2. Run `lean4export` on the produced `Challenge.olean` in a `landrun` sandbox that has:\n   189\t   - read access to the entire file system and write access to `/dev`\n   190\t3. Repeat the same build sandboxed and export sandboxed steps with `Solution`\n   191\t4. Verify that all declarations used in the statement of all relevant theorems in `Challenge`\n   192\t   are the same as in the `Solution` environment.\n   193\t   This always includes the declarations from `Init` with special meaning to the kernel. Both `Challenge`\n   194\t   and `Solution` therefore need to import the default prelude.\n   195\t5. Verify that the body of all relevant theorems in the `Solution` environment only uses axioms\n   196\t   listed in `permitted_axioms`\n   197\t6. Replay the `Solution` environment into the Lean kernel. Doing this within the same process as the\n   198\t   comparator should be safe as the worst thing that can happen at this point is an exploit that\n   199\t   makes the kernel accept when it should reject and that same exploit should also be applicable\n   200\t   from within an external process.\n   201\t\n   202\tNote that as `Challenge` is trusted, both the sandbox and lean4export step for `Challenge` are not\n   203\tnecessary to the best of our knowledge. We still adopt these rather free measures as additional\n   204\tparanoia in case an adversary comes up with a means of attack anyway.\n   205\t\n   206\t## Acknowledgement\n   207\tComparator was originally developed by Lean FRO, with feedback from the AIMO team, in support of the\n   208\tAIMO series of competitions and their goal of enabling trustworthy LLM Lean evaluation on Kaggle.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5aa49e59-911f-4515-a526-615b7175ef9d","uuid":"38588e66-a9c8-47bd-86ee-0874a363221d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:59:22.548Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>\n<feed xmlns:opensearch=\"http://a9.com/-/spec/opensearch/1.1/\" xmlns:arxiv=\"http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom\">\n  <id>https://arxiv.org/api/Tjad+Kq2i20MHJQ0natBbRp1msk</id>\n  <title>arXiv Query: search_query=&amp;id_list=2608.14673&amp;start=0&amp;max_results=10</title>\n  <updated>2026-11-05T05:59:12Z</updated>\n  <link href=\"https://arxiv.org/api/query?search_query=&amp;start=0&amp;max_results=10&amp;id_list=2608.14673\" type=\"application/atom+xml\"/>\n  <opensearch:itemsPerPage>10</opensearch:itemsPerPage>\n  <opensearch:totalResults>1</opensearch:totalResults>\n  <opensearch:startIndex>0</opensearch:startIndex>\n  <entry>\n    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2608.14673v3</id>\n    <title>A Human Audit of OpenAIs AI-Generated Mathematical Proofs</title>\n    <updated>2026-09-09T12:39:56Z</updated>\n    <link href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.14673v3\" rel=\"alternate\" type=\"text/html\"/>\n    <link href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.14673v3\" rel=\"related\" type=\"application/pdf\" title=\"pdf\"/>\n    <summary>We assess 18 chapter-specific reviews of the ten mathematical results announced by OpenAI on 1 August 2026, alongside review standards, Lean formalizations, subsequent research, and mathematical references. The article audits this review record without claiming a complete reconstruction of all ten proofs. No confirmed substantive mathematical error in a principal result remains in the examined assessments, although review depth varies and some dependencies remain partly checked. Chapter 8 presents the strongest reservation: a specialist review requests major revision of compressed analytic arguments. In Chapter 6, an apparent polarity error was withdrawn after an overbar lost during PDF extraction was recovered from the typeset source. Subsequent research independently reuses the Chapter 3 proof mechanism and confirms that Connes's rigidity conjecture is false, without independently reproducing Chapter 4's stronger infinite-family result. Among the cited follow-ups, Chapter 7 receives the strongest direct theorem-level corroboration through a stronger hardness theorem. Related equality results in Chapter 8 do not verify the analytic inequality proof. Some follow-ups disclose material AI assistance. We argue that confidence should combine formal checking, human reconstruction, independent mathematical use, and a public record supporting correction of both proofs and reviews.</summary>\n    <category term=\"cs.AI\" scheme=\"http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom\"/>\n    <category term=\"cs.GL\" scheme=\"http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom\"/>\n    <category term=\"quant-ph\" scheme=\"http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom\"/>\n    <published>2026-08-03T15:12:46Z</published>\n    <arxiv:comment>8 pages, 5 references. V2: 11 pages, 8 references. V3. 13 pages, 27 references. Revised and updated version</arxiv:comment>\n    <arxiv:primary_category term=\"cs.AI\"/>\n    <author>\n      <name>Mikołaj Sienicki</name>\n    </author>\n    <author>\n      <name>Krzysztof Sienicki</name>\n    </author>\n  </entry>\n</feed>\n----\n     1\t# Comparator\n     2\t## Deprecation\n     3\tThis project has been merged into core as of Lean release v4.35.0 in the form of `lake comparator`\n     4\tand the accompanying `lake check`. Users are advised to migrate their validations setups to these\n     5\ttools as they are the only ones guaranteed to receive further security and performance improvements.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Introduction\n     8\tComparator is a trustworthy judge for Lean proofs. It relies on having an existing Lean installation as\n     9\twell as:\n    10\t1. [`landrun`](https://github.com/Zouuup/landrun), compiled from the `main` branch's source, present in `PATH`\n    11\t2. [`lean4export`](https://github.com/leanprover/lean4export/), at a version that is compatible with whatever Lean version your project is targeting, present in `PATH`\n    12\t3. (optional) [nanoda](https://github.com/ammkrn/nanoda_lib/), compiled with a recent version of Rust.\n    13\t   This is only necessary if you want to check with the nanoda kernel in addition to the builtin one.\n    14\t   `cargo build --release` will place `nanoda_bin` in the `target/release` directory of the checked-out directory,\n    15\t   this directory must be present in `PATH`\n    16\t\n    17\t> [!NOTE]\n    18\t> Alternatively full paths to these binaries can be specified using the environment variables\n    19\t> `COMPARATOR_LANDRUN`, `COMPARATOR_LEAN4EXPORT`, and `COMPARATOR_NANODA` when invoking Comparator.\n    20\t\n    21\tComparator is configured through a JSON file:\n    22\t```\n    23\t{\n    24\t    \"challenge_module\": \"Challenge\",\n    25\t    \"solution_module\": \"Solution\",\n    26\t    \"theorem_names\": [\"todo1\"],\n    27\t    \"permitted_axioms\": [\"propext\", \"Quot.sound\", \"Classical.choice\"]\n    28\t}\n    29\t```\n    30\tWhere `Challenge.lean` contains at least a theorem named `todo1` that has a `sorry` (or any other proof)\n    31\tand `Solution.lean` is provided by a party trying to convince you that they have proven `todo1` by\n    32\twriting out the same theorem but with a proper proof attached.\n    33\t\n    34\tGiven the following assumptions:\n    35\t1. The transitive closure of imports of `Challenge.lean` as well as `lakefile.toml`/`lakefile.lean`\n    36\t   are controlled by you or trustworthy.\n    37\t2. You have not previously tried to compile the `Solution` file or any other potentially adversarial\n    38\t   files (as that might compromise your `Challenge` file to make it seem like you are looking for a\n    39\t   different proof than you actually are)\n    40\t3. You have the `landrun` and `lean4export` binary in `PATH`\n    41\t4. `landrun` works correctly on your system and `Solution.lean` does not\n    42\t   exploit any bugs in `landrun` that allow a process to escape its sandbox\n    43\t5. The Lean kernel is correct (with `external_kernels` this can be reduced to\n    44\t   \"At least one of the Lean kernel or the `external_kernels` is correct\")\n    45\t6. You are not running this under a privileged user\n    46\t\n    47\tIf the following command succeeds:\n    48\t```\n    49\tsystemd-run --property=RestrictAddressFamilies=~AF_UNIX --user --pty -E PATH=\"$PATH\" --working-directory $(pwd) -- bash -c 'lake env path/to/comparator/binary path/to/config.json'\n    50\t```\n    51\t\n    52\tAll theorems in `Solution` that are listed in `theorem_names` are guaranteed to:\n    53\t1. Prove the same statement as provided in `Challenge`\n    54\t2. Use no more axioms than listed in `permitted_axioms`\n    55\t3. Be accepted by the Lean kernel\n    56\t\n    57\t> [!NOTE]\n    58\t> The Trusted Code Base of Landrun naturally includes the operating system and hardware it is running on, plus its sandboxing mechanism.\n    59\t> The systemd-run part explicitly guard against a vulnerability in landrun, Comparator's current sandboxing solution, that will be fixed in Linux 7.1\n    60\t\n    61\tNote that running `lake exe cache get` to download a Mathlib cache is acceptable before running the\n    62\tcomparator if you trust the cache to not be modified as to, e.g. contain different definitions from\n    63\tthe one you would expect.\n    64\t\n    65\tFurthermore, it is possible to avoid trusting `landrun`'s ability to sandbox the `Solution.lean` file:\n    66\tif you have obtained a fully pre-built `.lake` directory through other means and without compromising your\n    67\tchecking environment, `Solution.lean` will not be rebuilt.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Checking with Additional Kernels\n    70\tComparator can additionally check solutions with external kernels. To do this you must register them\n    71\tin the `external_kernels` list:\n    72\t```\n    73\t{\n    74\t    \"challenge_module\": \"Challenge\",\n    75\t    \"solution_module\": \"Solution\",\n    76\t    \"theorem_names\": [\"todo1\"],\n    77\t    \"permitted_axioms\": [\"propext\", \"Quot.sound\", \"Classical.choice\"],\n    78\t    \"external_kernels\": {\n    79\t        \"mykernel\": [\"kernel_bin\", \"--threads=4\", \"--paranoid\"]\n    80\t    }\n    81\t}\n    82\t```\n    83\tComparator will execute the command described by the `mykernel` array and additionally pass a\n    84\tfile, containing the solution export to the kernel, in this case: `kernel_bin --threads=4 --paranoid export.ndjson`\n    85\t\n    86\tFor backwards compatibility reasons users may instead set `enable_nanoda: true` to obtain a config\n    87\tthat calls `nanoda_bin`. Furthermore, comparator currently attempts to detect `nanoda`-style kernels\n    88\tby checking whether the name contains the string `noda` and instead passing a `nanoda`-style\n    89\t`config.json` to them. This is only intended as a migration path while the kernel ecosystem\n    90\tmoves toward having an option to receive the input file as a `CLI` argument.\n    91\t\n    92\tFor development purposes, comparator supports overriding `nanoda` specifically using the\n    93\t`COMPARATOR_NANODA` environment variable.\n    94\t## Definition Holes\n    95\tSometimes challenges want to leave open definitions for solutions to fill in. This can range from\n    96\tsimple things like filling in a `Prop` valued definition to resolve whether a conjecture is true or\n    97\tfalse, all the way to constructing complex mathematical objects. For these types of solutions,\n    98\tcomparator can guarantee that:\n    99\t1. They use no more axioms than listed in `permitted_axioms`\n   100\t2. They are accepted by the Lean kernel\n   101\t3. The name, type, universe levels and safety levels of all definition holes match\n   102\t\n   103\tCrucially, many definition hole challenges can be gamed without additional oversight.\n   104\tFor example, given a conjecture-style challenge:\n   105\t```lean\n   106\tdef ChallengeSolution : Prop := sorry\n   107\ttheorem challenge : RiemannHypothesis ↔ ChallengeSolution := sorry\n   108\t```\n   109\ta solution could define `ChallengeSolution` as:\n   110\t```lean\n   111\tdef ChallengeSolution : Prop := RiemannHypothesis\n   112\t```\n   113\tand conduct a simple proof of `challenge` by reflexivity. The intention of the challenge though was\n   114\tof course to ask for a `True` or `False` value for `ChallengeSolution`. For this reason, all\n   115\tdefinition hole solutions **must** always be checked with an additional (potentially human)\n   116\tverifier.\n   117\t\n   118\tTo establish a definition hole, the challenge must provide it as a sorried definition:\n   119\t```lean\n   120\tdef large : Nat := sorry\n   121\t\n   122\ttheorem large_lt : 37 < large := sorry\n   123\t```\n   124\tAll of the holes must then be put into the `definition_names` field in `configuration.json`:\n   125\t```\n   126\t{\n   127\t    \"challenge_module\": \"Challenge\",\n   128\t    \"solution_module\": \"Solution\",\n   129\t    \"theorem_names\": [\"large_lt\"],\n   130\t    \"definition_names\": [\"large\"],\n   131\t    \"permitted_axioms\": [\"propext\", \"Quot.sound\", \"Classical.choice\"]\n   132\t}\n   133\t```\n   134\tFor all `definition_names`, comparator ensures that in the solution:\n   135\t- the name, type, universe levels and safety level match\n   136\t- the constant does not (transitively) refer to non-permitted axioms\n   137\t- the constant type checks\n   138\t\n   139\tThus, the following solution would be accepted:\n   140\t```lean\n   141\tdef large : Nat := 38\n   142\t\n   143\ttheorem large_lt : 37 < large := by decide\n   144\t```\n   145\t\n   146\t## Development\n   147\t\n   148\tThe `scripts/fake-landrun.sh` can be used to replace Landrun in development if you are not on a Linux system that supports landrun.\n   149\t\n   150\tThe following commands, starting from the root directory of a fresh git checkout, will build and run `comparator` on one of the test examples:\n   151\t\n   152\t```sh\n   153\tlake build lean4export comparator\n   154\t\n   155\tcd tests/projects/simple_mismatch\n   156\t\n   157\tcat > lakefile.toml <<EOF\n   158\tname = \"comparatortest\"\n   159\tversion = \"0.1.0\"\n   160\t\n   161\t[[lean_lib]]\n   162\tname = \"Solution\"\n   163\t\n   164\t[[lean_lib]]\n   165\tname = \"Challenge\"\n   166\tEOF\n   167\t\n   168\tCOMPARATOR_LANDRUN=$(realpath ../../../scripts/fake-landrun.sh) COMPARATOR_LEAN4EXPORT=$(realpath ../../../.lake/packages/lean4export/.lake/build/bin/lean4export) lake env ../../../.lake/build/bin/comparator config.json\n   169\t```\n   170\t\n   171\tThe following commands, starting from the root directory of a fresh git checkout, will build and run the tests:\n   172\t\n   173\t```sh\n   174\tlake build lean4export comparator\n   175\tCOMPARATOR_LANDRUN=$(realpath scripts/fake-landrun.sh) COMPARATOR_LEAN4EXPORT=$(realpath .lake/packages/lean4export/.lake/build/bin/lean4export) lean --run runtests.lean\n   176\t```\n   177\t\n   178\tReplace the `landrun` and `lean4export` arguments as needed, or place the binaries in `PATH`.\n   179\t\n   180\t## Internals\n   181\tWe generally adopt a policy of not loading olean files as they just get mmaped into our address\n   182\tspace and then dereferenced and are as such a potential point of attack for sophisticated adversaries.\n   183\t\n   184\tThe comparator performs the following steps to ensure these properties:\n   185\t1. Build `Challenge` using `lake` in a `landrun` sandbox that has:\n   186\t   - read access to the entire file system and write access to `/dev`\n   187\t   - write access to the `.lake` directory of the project\n   188\t2. Run `lean4export` on the produced `Challenge.olean` in a `landrun` sandbox that has:\n   189\t   - read access to the entire file system and write access to `/dev`\n   190\t3. Repeat the same build sandboxed and export sandboxed steps with `Solution`\n   191\t4. Verify that all declarations used in the statement of all relevant theorems in `Challenge`\n   192\t   are the same as in the `Solution` environment.\n   193\t   This always includes the declarations from `Init` with special meaning to the kernel. Both `Challenge`\n   194\t   and `Solution` therefore need to import the default prelude.\n   195\t5. Verify that the body of all relevant theorems in the `Solution` environment only uses axioms\n   196\t   listed in `permitted_axioms`\n   197\t6. Replay the `Solution` environment into the Lean kernel. Doing this within the same process as the\n   198\t   comparator should be safe as the worst thing that can happen at this point is an exploit that\n   199\t   makes the kernel accept when it should reject and that same exploit should also be applicable\n   200\t   from within an external process.\n   201\t\n   202\tNote that as `Challenge` is trusted, both the sandbox and lean4export step for `Challenge` are not\n   203\tnecessary to the best of our knowledge. We still adopt these rather free measures as additional\n   204\tparanoia in case an adversary comes up with a means of attack anyway.\n   205\t\n   206\t## Acknowledgement\n   207\tComparator was originally developed by Lean FRO, with feedback from the AIMO team, in support of the\n   208\tAIMO series of competitions and their goal of enabling trustworthy LLM Lean evaluation on Kaggle.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HL3i4gamZhiuJotofKKcNT","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
