stive search diminish as k\n311:the graph and local code by deterministic exhaustive searches. The graph is the bipartite double\n395:exhaustive search. To motivate the algorithm, suppose we were given a partition of the variables\n411:Computing each Ng by exhaustive search within its group examines a total of at most\n705:bit operations and polynomial space. The finitely many smaller inputs are handled by exhaustive\n2210:If r < 2 or Q ⩽ R, use the exhaustive-search fallback below before forming τ , ϵ, or the parity\n2324:where qG < 2m < 2µ+1 . Exhaustively enumerating the shift tuples and checking (6.2) therefore finds\n2480:by exhaustive search. For n ⩾ 20, we next select the sparsification parameter. Define\n2487:If s < 2, we use exhaustive search; λn /νn2 tends to infinity, so there are only finitely many such\n2509:The finite exhaustive-search cases are absorbed in the constants. All parameters, initial checks, and\n3804:[Wil13]   Ryan Williams. Improving Exhaustive Search Implies Superpolynomial Lower Bounds. SIAM Journal on\n----GITIGNORE\n# Paper 3: the sandboxed replay's full logs are large and regenerable; their\n# hashes and the short extracts (axioms.txt, versions, the last 200 lines)\n# are committed by item 01.\nreplay/logs/*.log\nreplay/logs/*.jsonl\nreplay/logs/*.export\nreplay/logs/*.out\n# LaTeX build products (main.pdf is committed, as paper 1's is)\nlatex/*.aux\nlatex/*.bbl\nlatex/*.blg\nlatex/*.fdb_latexmk\nlatex/*.fls\nlatex/*.log\nlatex/*.out\nlatex/*.toc\nlatex/*.synctex.gz\n----MAKE\n# Builds paper3/latex/main.pdf — paper 3 (title fixed by the owner, verbatim in main.tex).\n#   make          latexmk -pdf (pdflatex + bibtex as often as needed)\n#   make check    fail on undefined references or citations in the last build\n#   make clean    remove build products but the PDF\n# Figures are produced by the scripts under ../scripts/ into figures/;\n# tables likewise into tables/ (item 02 and item 06 write them).\nall: main.pdf\n\nmain.pdf: main.tex $(wildcard sec*.tex) refs.bib $(wildcard tables/*.tex) $(wildcard figures/*.pdf)\n\tlatexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error main.tex\n\ncheck: main.pdf\n\t@! grep -E \"undefined|Citation .* undefined|Reference .* undefined\" main.log\n\t@echo \"no undefined references; $$(pdfinfo main.pdf | grep Pages)\"\n\nclean:\n\tlatexmk -c\n\trm -f *.bbl *.blg *.synctex.gz\n\ndistclean: clean\n\trm -f main.pdf\n\n.PHONY: all check clean distclean\n----SEC3\n\\label{sec:beyond}\n\nThe manuscript \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nis 67 pages; the comparator statement is 220 lines. Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\nread the statement. This section reads the manuscript's claims one by one\nand says, for each, whether it is what the Lean theorem asserts, whether it\nfollows from the Lean theorem by an argument that exists only on paper, or\nwhether it rests on a bound that the Lean library does not contain. The\ncollection's own scope note draws the line in two sentences, which we quote\nin full (\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, lines 9 and 11):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\\sloppy\n``For fixed $k \\ge 2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly\ncounts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$\nvariables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$,\nwhere $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has\nvisited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.''\n\n``For $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m \\le Dn$ for\nfixed $D > 0$, this improves to\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.''\n\\end{quote}\nEverything below is an elaboration of that note against the manuscript's\ntext and the library's files. Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} is the summary.\n\n\\subsection{Three statuses, and what ``paper-only'' means}\n\\label{sec:beyond-statuses}\n\nWe tag every claim with one of three words.\n\n\\begin{itemize}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5aa49e59-911f-4515-a526-615b7175ef9d","uuid":"22a2deeb-f652-4cee-bcd1-6b9fe2d9ca57","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:00:11.083Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"----IP\n3666:[BI13]    Chris Beck and Russell Impagliazzo. Strong ETH Holds for Regular Resolution. 45th ACM Symposium on\n3684:[CIP06]   Chris Calabro, Russell Impagliazzo, and Ramamohan Paturi. A Duality between Clause Width and Clause\n3686:[CGI+ 16] Marco L. Carmosino, Jiawei Gao, Russell Impagliazzo, Ivan Mihajlin, Ramamohan Paturi, and Stefan\n3731:[IMP12]  Russell Impagliazzo, William Matthews, and Ramamohan Paturi. A Satisfiability Algorithm for AC 0 . 23rd\n3733:[IP01]   Russell Impagliazzo and Ramamohan Paturi. On the Complexity of k-SAT . Journal of Computer and\n3735:[IPZ01]  Russell Impagliazzo, Ramamohan Paturi, and Francis Zane. Which Problems Have Strongly Exponential\n----IP01entry\n3733: [IP01]   Russell Impagliazzo and Ramamohan Paturi. On the Complexity of k-SAT . Journal of Computer and\n3734:          System Sciences 62(2), 2001, 367–375. 1, 4\n3735: [IPZ01]  Russell Impagliazzo, Ramamohan Paturi, and Francis Zane. Which Problems Have Strongly Exponential\n3736:          Complexity? Journal of Computer and System Sciences 63(4), 2001, 512–530. 1, 5, 6, 18\n3737: [JMV13] Hamid Jahanjou, Eric Miles, and Emanuele Viola. Local Reductions. Electronic Colloquium on Compu-\n3738:          tational Complexity, Report TR13-099, revision 2, 2013. Conference version in ICALP 2015, 749–760.\n3739:          51\n3740: [JVV86]  Mark R. Jerrum, Leslie G. Valiant, and Vijay V. Vazirani. Random Generation of Combinatorial Structures\n3733:[IP01]   Russell Impagliazzo and Ramamohan Paturi. On the Complexity of k-SAT . Journal of Computer and\n3735:[IPZ01]  Russell Impagliazzo, Ramamohan Paturi, and Francis Zane. Which Problems Have Strongly Exponential\n----PRIOR\n153:Experiments on formulas from cir