{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y1ToHgsrie2qzCQufk8wfW","type":"tool_result","content":"----HIST18\n- *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False.* The paper gives a deterministic algorithm that exactly counts satisfying assignments of fixed-width CNF formulas in subexponential time and polynomial space. The case of 3-SAT refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis. An independent working group of computer scientists led by Russell Impagliazzo, Ramamohan Paturi, and Ryan Williams will examine and explain the proof. At the group’s request, we are making the materials public immediately so others can examine them as well.\n\n----HISTOTHER\n18:- *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False.* The paper gives a deterministic algorithm that exactly counts satisfying assignments of fixed-width CNF formulas in subexponential time and polynomial \n27:- *The maximum number of mutually unbiased bases in dimension six.* We restored the C++ source and saved numerical records for the computation that excludes a fourth mutually unbiased basis. A Pyth\n----SEC5nanoda\nupstream and not patched.\n\n\\subsection{The second kernel}\n\nThe comparator's JSON for the family has \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}:\nOpenAI's check of this family ran the Lean kernel only. We ran a second\nkernel, in the same sandbox, after the build\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/nanoda.sh}, recorded in the README before it\nran): \\texttt{lean4export} (tag \\texttt{v4.34.0}, commit\n\\texttt{076e8e57}, built with the pinned \\texttt{v4.34.1} toolchain so\nthat it reads the object files the build produced) exported\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and its transitive dependencies from the\nbuilt environment---2,355,666,449 bytes of NDJSON, 43,633,742 lines,\nformat version 3.1.0, Lean githash \\texttt{5045d005…}, in 68~seconds---and\n\\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} \\citep{Nanoda} (version 0.4.31, commit\n\\texttt{8de7e0c4}, 2026-10-29; an external type checker for Lean~4\nwritten in Rust, which shares no code with the Lean kernel) checked that\nexport with \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice} and\n\\texttt{Quot.sound} as the permitted axioms and the \\texttt{Nat} and\n\\texttt{String} literal extensions on. Its output\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/nanoda_check.txt}):\n\\begin{quote}\n\\texttt{Checked 95176 declarations with no errors}\n\\end{quote}\nin 110~seconds on eight cores, exit code~0, after printing back the\ntheorem's statement and the three axioms from its own parse of the\nexport. The export contains exactly three \\texttt{axiom} declarations\n(\\texttt{grep -c '\"axiom\"'} on the file), so nothing else could have been\npermitted. The export file (SHA-256 \\texttt{bdbc9afe…},\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/export_sha256.txt}) stays in the\nsandbox directory by size; the configuration, the build logs and the\n----sec1intro\n% Introduction -- drafted by loop0015 item 09.\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 09 drafts this section: the title's sentence made precise in the first paragraph (a guarantee, not a running time; existential constants never computed; $\\rhon > n$ for every $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$; the sharper exponents unverified); the one-paragraph summary; what is claimed and what is not; the stance (a careful reading by people who checked; no flaw found). Ends with what this does not claim''.}\n----SEC10\npaper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex:332:  \\citep{Nanoda}. Section~\\ref{sec:related} records the versions.\npaper3/latex/sec2_statement.tex:327:is related to \\texttt{runtimeScale} by","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5aa49e59-911f-4515-a526-615b7175ef9d","uuid":"f83119e1-e433-4e51-b2e5-13abde014a46","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:00:14.152Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"----HIST18\n- *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False.* The paper gives a deterministic algorithm that exactly counts satisfying assignments of fixed-width CNF formulas in subexponential t