entence matter for us. First, the group it names is","+led by two of the three authors of the papers that stated the hypotheses","+(Section~\\ref{sec:related-hypotheses}). Second, the verb is ``will'': the","+log announces an examination to come. Nothing from that group is in the","+clone, and we fetched nothing from it, so this paper has no finding of","+theirs to cite, agree with or disagree with. Whatever that group","+publishes about the proof will carry more weight on the proof than","+Section~\\ref{sec:proof} does, which only inspected and replayed. Third,","+the log says ``the paper gives \\ldots{} subexponential time'' with no","+exponent; the exponent is in the scope note.","+","+That scope note is \\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, seventeen lines, quoted","+in full in Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}. It is the collection's own statement","+of the gap between what Lean proves (the exponent with the squared","+$\\log\\log$ factor) and what the manuscript claims (two sharper","+exponents). Its table (line 17) links the result to the one comparator","+statement, \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}.","+So the collection itself records that the verified exponent is the weaker","+one. What it does not record is what the weaker exponent means at any","+size: that $\\rhon$, the verified exponent, is larger than $n$ for every","+$n$ between $50$ and $e^{5504}$, so that the verified bound with constant","+one sits above $2^n$ at every size anyone will ever run","+(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). That is the title's observation, and the","+note does not make it.","+","+The third document is \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/README.md},","+ten lines. It says how the collection's checks are run: ``Install","+\\texttt{comparator}, \\texttt{landrun}, and \\texttt{lean4export}, and make","+them available on \\texttt{PATH}.'' (line 3), then from the \\texttt{lean/}","+directory \\texttt{lake update}, \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, and","+\\texttt{lake env comparator} on a family's JSON (lines 7--9; the example","+is another family's). The JSON for this family has six fields","+(Section~\\ref{sec:related-comparator} reads them).","+","+\\subsection{The two papers that stated the hypotheses}","+\\label{sec:related-hypotheses}","+","+ETH, the Exponential Time Hypothesis, says that 3-SAT (deciding whether a","+formula whose clauses have three literals each can be satisfied) has no","+algorithm whose running time grows slower than every exponential in the","+number of variables. SETH, the Strong form, says that as the clause width","+grows, the best possible base of the exponential tends to $2$, the base of","+trying every assignment. Both are from \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}; the","+standard setting of SETH, and the lemma that lets one move between the","+number of variables and the number of clauses, are from","+\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}. The manuscript cites both as","+\\texttt{[IP01]} and \\texttt{[IPZ01]} (extract page 66, lines 3733--3736)","+and states each hypothesis in its own words in its \\S1.1.1, which","+Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} quotes with page and line. We do not hold","+either paper: \\repofile{literature/} has no file for them, and","+\\repofile{literature/MISSING.md} now records both with their DOIs. Every","+statement of a hypothesis in this paper is therefore the manuscript's","+statement of it, not the original's, and we quote nothing from the two","+originals. The manuscript's own account of what motivated SETH is one","+sentence we can locate (page 4, lines 170--171): the improvements of the","+known algorithms ``over exhaustive search diminish as $k$ grows [IP01,","+Open Problems, item 2]''. Exhaustive search is the ``trying all $2^n$","+assignments'' of the title.","+","+\\subsection{A human audit of an earlier batch of results}","+\\label{sec:related-audit}","+","+\\citet{SienickiSienicki2026} is a published human audit of OpenAI's","+AI-generated proofs. We fetched its arXiv abstract page and API record","+(v1 of 3 August 2026, v2 of 25 August, v3 of 9 September 2026; 13 pages;","+primary class cs.AI). It is not about this family. Its abstract says","+what it covers: ``We assess 18 chapter-specific reviews of the ten","+mathematical results announced by OpenAI on 1 August 2026, alongside","+review standards, Lean formalizations, subsequent research, and","+mathematical references.'' The family this paper reads is dated 17","+October 2026, revised 25 October, and entered the collection on 26","+October (Section~\\ref{sec:related-collection}); it is not one of the ten,","+and the abstract mentions neither it nor ETH (the script checks). So we","+cite the audit for its method of review and for nothing about the proof.","+","+Four sentences of the abstract bear on how this paper was written. On","+restraint: ``The article audits this review record without claiming a","+complete reconstruction of all ten proofs.'' On the finding: ``No","+confirmed substantive mathematical error in a principal result remains in","+the examined assessments, although review depth varies and some","+dependencies remain partly checked.'' On a hazard of reading proofs","+through text extraction: ``In Chapter 6, an apparent polarity error was","+withdrawn after an overbar lost during PDF extraction was recovered from","+the typeset source.'' And on what confidence should rest on: ``We argue","+that confidence should combine formal checking, human reconstruction,","+independent mathematical use, and a public record supporting correction","+of both proofs and reviews.''","+","+The third of these is a warning we took. This paper reads the manuscript","+through a \\texttt{pdftotext} extract, the same kind of text in which that","+overbar was lost. That is why every quotation from the manuscript carries","+the page of the PDF beside the line of the extract, why the Lean is read","+from its source files and never from a rendering, and why no claim in","+this paper rests on a formula read from the extract alone: the formulas","+that matter are the Lean's, re-typed in Section~\\ref{sec:statement} from","+the \\texttt{.lean} file and re-checked by the kernel in","+Section~\\ref{sec:lean}. Against the fourth sentence, this paper is one of","+the four things it lists, formal checking, replayed by us and by a","+second kernel, together with a reading of what the checked statement","+says. It is not a human reconstruction of the proof: Section~\\ref{sec:proof}","+inspected the proof's files and did not re-derive the argument. We","+fetched the abstract only, and quote nothing from the paper's body.","+","+\\subsection{The comparator}","+\\label{sec:related-comparator}","+","+The comparator is the program the collection used to check that the","+solution file proves the challenge statement","+\\citep{LeanComparator}. We fetched its README from the","+\\texttt{master} branch of \\texttt{leanprover/comparator} on 2026-11-05","+(latest commit \\texttt{865edc14} of 2026-10-14, ``doc: migration","+notice''; the repository is archived and under the Apache-2.0 licence).","+Its first lines say the tool has moved: ``This project has been merged","+into core as of Lean release v4.35.0 in the form of \\texttt{lake","+comparator} and the accompanying \\texttt{lake check}'' (line 3). The","+collection pins Lean v4.34.1 (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}), one release","+earlier, so the stand-alone tool is the one its README names. The README","+opens: ``Comparator is a trustworthy judge for Lean proofs.'' (line 8).","+","+What it checks is stated as three guarantees for the theorems a JSON","+configuration names (lines 52--55): ``All theorems in \\texttt{Solution}","+that are listed in \\texttt{theorem\\_names} are guaranteed to: 1. Prove","+the same statement as provided in \\texttt{Challenge} 2. Use no more","+axioms than listed in \\texttt{permitted\\_axioms} 3. Be accepted by the","+Lean kernel''. In plain words: the solution proves exactly the sentence","+the challenge posed, uses only the agreed axioms (the three standard ones","+in the example configuration, line 27), and passes the kernel, the small","+core of Lean that checks proofs. How it checks them is in its","+``Internals'' (lines 184--200), six steps: build the challenge inside a","+sandbox; export its declarations with \\texttt{lean4export}; do the same","+for the solution; ``Verify that all declarations used in the statement of","+all relevant theorems in \\texttt{Challenge} are the same as in the","+\\texttt{Solution} environment'' (lines 191--192); ``Verify that the body","+of all relevant theorems in the \\texttt{Solution} environment only uses","+axioms listed in \\texttt{permitted\\_axioms}'' (lines 195--196); and","+``Replay the \\texttt{Solution} environment into the Lean kernel'' (line","+197). The first guarantee is therefore a match of the statement's","+declarations as exported, not a textual comparison of the two files.","+That is the check Section~\\ref{sec:proof} says we did not reproduce: our","+statement match is textual (Table~\\ref{tab:trust-boundary}, row (iv)).","+","+The guarantees hold under six stated assumptions (lines 34--45). The one","+that concerns the kernel is the fifth: ``The Lean kernel is correct (with","+\\texttt{external\\_kernels} this can be reduced to `At least one of the","+Lean kernel or the \\texttt{external\\_kernels} is correct')'' (lines","+43--44). The sandbox is \\texttt{landrun} (line 10), a tool that confines","+the build so that a malicious solution file cannot tamper with the","+checker; the README notes that its trusted base ``naturally includes the","+operating system and hardware it is running on, plus its sandboxing","+mechanism'' (line 58). External kernels are optional: ``Comparator can","+additionally check solutions with external kernels'' (line 70), and ``For","+backwards compatibility reasons users may instead set","+\\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: true} to obtain a config that calls","+\\texttt{nanoda\\_bin}'' (lines 86--87). The tool ``was originally","+developed by Lean FRO, with feedback from the AIMO team, in support of","+the AIMO series of competitions'' (lines 207--208).","+","+For this family the configuration,","+\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}, has six","+fields: the challenge module, the solution module","+\\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, one theorem name","+(\\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}), an empty","+\\texttt{definition\\_names}, the three standard axioms as","+\\texttt{permitted\\_axioms}, and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}; there is","+no \\texttt{external\\_kernels} field. So the check the collection","+configured for this family is the Lean kernel alone, under the fifth","+assumption in its unweakened form. Section~\\ref{sec:replay} is the","+weakened form, supplied by us: the same theorem's dependencies through a","+second kernel. Our \\texttt{bwrap} sandbox there played the role","+\\texttt{landrun} plays here, for the same reason, and we did not run the","+comparator itself.","+","+\\subsection{nanoda}","+\\label{sec:related-nanoda}","+","+nanoda is the second checker \\citep{Nanoda}. Its README, fetched from","+the \\texttt{master} branch of \\texttt{ammkrn/nanoda\\_lib} on 2026-11-05,","+says what it is in one sentence (line 3): ``This is an external type","+checker for the Lean 4 programming language and theorem prover.'' The","+repository's description line is ``Library implementing type","+inference/checking functionality based on the Lean theorem prover''; the","+licence is Apache-2.0. It reads the file \\texttt{lean4export} writes:","+``Export files can be created with lean4export'' (line 13), and ``Given","+that lean4export already uses json as its export format, adding a json","+parser as a dependency doesn't increase the overall complexity of the","+project'' (line 23). Its axiom rule is the comparator's: ``The average","+Lean user will want to permit the three semi-official axioms, which are","+\\texttt{Quot.sound}, \\texttt{Classical.choice}, and \\texttt{propext}, and","+most export files will also rely on \\texttt{Lean.trustCompiler}'' (line","+33); the export we checked contains the first three and no fourth","+(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}). Two kernel extensions for arithmetic on","+literals are off by default and ``enable or disable the Nat and String","+kernel extensions'' (line 39); the replay turned both on.","+","+The latest commit of the repository at the time of the fetch is","+\\texttt{8de7e0c4} of 2026-10-29, and that is the commit the replay built","+and ran (version 0.4.31; the script checks the replay's log against the","+fetched record). The checker is written in Rust and shares no code with","+the Lean kernel, which is what makes it a second opinion. Its author's","+book on external type checkers, linked from line 3 of the README, was not","+fetched.","+","+\\subsection{Where we looked for an earlier statement of the title's observation}","+\\label{sec:related-search}","+","+The title says the verified guarantee is weaker than trying all $2^n$","+assignments at every practical size. We looked for an earlier statement","+of that observation in the texts this paper rests on, and in no others:","+the collection's \\clonefile{history.md}, its scope note","+\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, the abstract of","+\\citet{SienickiSienicki2026}, the comparator README, the nanoda README,","+and the manuscript extract. The script searches each for the words such","+a statement would be likely to use (``practical'', ``brute'', ``trying","+all'', ``exhaustive'', ``$2^n$'', ``constants are'', ``concrete''). Outside","+the manuscript, none occurs. In the manuscript, ``practical'' occurs once","+(page 4, line 153), in a sentence about the practical value of SAT","+solvers on industrial formulas, a different sense; ``exhaustive'' occurs","+eleven times, as the motivation of SETH quoted above (line 170) and as the","+proof's own fallback for small inputs (lines 2480 and 2487, where the","+algorithm uses ``exhaustive search'' below the cutoff","+$n \\ge 20$). The nearest earlier statements are the manuscript's own","+sentence that Lean verifies ``the weaker exponent'' (page 2, lines","+63--67; Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) and the scope note: both say the","+verified exponent is the weaker one, and neither says at what sizes the","+verified bound exceeds $2^n$, which is the title's point. Within these","+texts we found no prior statement of it. The search is bounded by that","+list: we did not search the literature at large, the working group's","+output, or the press.","+","+\\subsection{What this does not claim}","+\\label{sec:related-not}","+","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the working group's findings.} The","+  collection's log says the group ``will examine and explain the","+  proof''; we hold nothing it has written, and we neither anticipate","+  nor contradict it.","+\\item \\textbf{The human audit does not cover this family.} It is cited","+  for its method and its stated lesson about PDF extraction; its body","+  was not fetched, and no sentence of it beyond the abstract is quoted.","+\\item \\textbf{The two hypothesis papers were not read.} They are cited","+  as the manuscript cites them, and the hypotheses are stated as the","+  manuscript states them (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}).","+\\item \\textbf{We did not run the comparator.} What we reproduced is","+  stated in Section~\\ref{sec:proof}'s Table~\\ref{tab:trust-boundary};","+  the comparator's statement match by exported declarations is not among","+  it.","+\\item \\textbf{No claim that landrun, lean4export or nanoda is correct.}","+  Each is quoted for what its README says it does; the replay's","+  evidence is that two independent checkers agreed on this export, and","+  the trust left in \\texttt{lean4export} is stated in","+  Section~\\ref{sec:replay}.","+\\item \\textbf{``No prior statement'' is bounded by the list above.} Six","+  texts, read with a script; not a literature search.","+\\end{itemize}","+\\endgroup"]}],"originalFile":"% Related work and context -- drafted by loop0015 item 08.\n\\section{Related work and context}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 08 drafts this section: the human audit of the August 2026 results (arXiv 2608.14673), the working group named in the collection's history, Impagliazzo--Paturi, Impagliazzo--Paturi--Zane, the comparator mechanism, nanoda; ``we found no prior statement'', never ``first''. Ends with what this does not claim''.}\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017ZC3gwz5G245AqPfeUVLUj","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
